A||N||18||WhiteFieroBlur||Snow tomorrow what should I buy?||||5||
Z||000000||WhiteFieroBlur||11-27-2001||02:13 PM||||Well I should have prepared but oh well. We don't get just snow here we get ice and last year I remember my car was totally under inches of ice completely. What should I do to prevent my car having so much ice or make it easy for me to get the ice off so I don't spend hours before I goto work making my car drivable. I am gonna goto the auto parts store and buy some stuff. its sure gonna be intersting tomorrow morning when I goto work.||||reg||5
Z||000001||MinnGreenGT||11-27-2001||02:17 PM||||You definitely need a good layer of Rain-X on the glass... that makes ice removal much easier.  If you have a place to store a tarp/cover when not in use- I'd guess that'd be nice (maybe more of hassle than it's worth though).  Also a remote starter may seem like a luxury, but after the wet snow/ice we recieved here last night- it was a necessity (as well as remote door locks for when the locks ice over).<P>Best of luck to you!<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||11-27-2001||02:27 PM||||You need to buy a winter car and find a place to store your Fiero!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Anyways, this is what I do to <I>any</I> car that's going to be driven in the winter:<P>Very good wax job!  Snow and ice will come off the car easier.  Sometimes you'll need to find a heated garage or somewhere where you can wax it again.  The more frequent you do the better.<P>EDIT: MinnGreen beat me to the rain-x part!<P>Use Rain-x on the exterior glass.  Do about 3 coats if you don't use the product regularly.  I have found that ice will snap off a lot easier when you scrape it if you have rain-x on.<P>I spray armor all (I have a gallon of it I aas given and dont use it for interior or anything except this) on the mudflaps and inner wheel wells to keep the snow goobers from forming.  It seems to help, but you need to reapply often.<P>Get some good premium washer solvent.  Usually about this time the good stuff is on sale.  I think currently at a Murray's Auto Parts around here there is the orange Rain-X winter solvent on sale for $1 a gallon.  The premium stuff usually has a higher concentration of some type of alcohol or whatever it is that lowers the freezing point of water.<P>A good ice scraper and snow brush.  Definatly a must.<P>Got some good gloves to keep in the car?<P>Another thing to check is take your car to a place that can do a full test on your battery.  If your battery has a bad cell, it might not show during the summer but once the cold comes you'll know its there, because the car won't even crank.<P>Make sure to spray your car off frequently too during the winter!  Though I don't drive my Fiero in the winter, whatever car/truck I'm driving I usually take it to one of the spray off places where you do it yourself at least once a week.  Make sure to spray the underside very very well.<p>[This message has been edited by FieroJoe (edited 11-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||11-27-2001||02:33 PM||||Also, forgot some things!<P>What I do to prevent the locks freezing up is use Lock-EEZ.  Its a graphite type lubricant except that it is in a alcohol carrier so it penetrates into the lock, and then the alcohol evaporates so you're left with a good coating of graphite.  In addition to keeping the locks un-frozen, it will also help prevent the build up of salt in the locks, which can seize them permanently.<P>Another good thing to do is to lube all over your door hinges and hood hinges.  WD-40 or anything handy will work great.  I prefer Tri-flow, which is a very good lubricant/pentrating oil in every aspect.  It has teflon in it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||MinnGreenGT||11-27-2001||02:45 PM||||I agree with FieroJoe on all his stuff... especially the winter vehicle thing (hide the Fiero from winter).<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/bravada01.jpg"> <BR>This is my winter vehicle ('94 Olds Bravada)<BR>You can't beat all wheel drive with SmartTrak<BR>BTW: I <B>just</B> took this photo- it's still nasty outside!||||reg||1
Z||000005||84Bill||11-27-2001||02:45 PM||||Covering the car is a good idea. Here in PA you can be fined for chunks flying off your car. I'll warm the car for about 15 to 20 minutes before I leave <P>Just FYI<BR>I keep a blanket, water and some pretzel rods in my car year round. Thing that I make sure I have for impending snow are a cellphone, 5lbs bag of salt, blanket, asprin (hate to be stranded and have a headache) Flashlight, jumper cables, 1 gal antifreeze, something to read, basic food and water and some basic tools.<BR>I have had occasion where I stupidly ran out of gas in subzero temps and was very glad to have that blanket keeping my tootsies warm while I awaited rescue.<BR> <BR>I may be right outside Philadelphia but when the roads are jammed with stuck traffic and or impassable it is wise to be prepared for a long wait.<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 11-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||WhiteFieroBlur||11-27-2001||02:51 PM||||Well I just looked outside and my cars already covered in snow, I thought it was gonna snow tomorrow. Grrrr. Oh well hopefully it will stop soon. Sure is coming down fast. Well looks like I met my first day of winter. Thanx for all the info, hopefully I'll be able to free it of snow and stuff later on and do the things I need to, to it.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fierospeeder||11-27-2001||02:55 PM||||heres a more hp way of doing it.<P><BR>go to harbor freights website and buy their propane tank attachment.  Its a propane torch that is used to melt the snow off the sidewalk.<P>NOTE:  But i take no responsibility if you melt your car.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by fierospeeder (edited 11-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fformula88||11-27-2001||03:30 PM||||I'd be less concerned with snow/ice on the car and more with driving in and on it.  Get yourself a good scraper.  The best ones are the ones with the brass scraper end.  They are a hundred times better than a plastic one.  That will get the windows clean.  Get a snow brush to brush the snow off the hood, roof etc.  But be careful as you do not want to scratch the finish.<P>The best advice if you drive on lots of ice... get some good ice tires!  Studs work the best but there are other good ones.  Blizacks are good in the ice, and I think Yokohama makes a tire with ground walnut shells in it to bite into the ice.<P>I guess I don't get too concerned with snow/ice on the car since its impossible to keep it off for four months of the year in Buffalo!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||NEPTUNE||11-27-2001||03:31 PM||||I usually wax mine. <BR>(sorry, I couldn't help myself. Its 80 degrees here today).||||reg||6
